- P-55 **A size may hold arithmetic, compared by its written form once numerals are folded (array fork 1, 15a with (b)).** The checker accepts a sum, difference, product or power in a size. `2 3` folds to `6`, but `s0 s1` is not `s1 s0`. The class loader computes the value when it makes a class. A size name may equal a numeral, as the team's Q&A answers, so the `N[\0\]` arm of a `typecase N[\b0\]` is reachable. The library keeps the team's sizes written as products and sums.
  from: explorations/coordinator/positions-audit/trial/evidence/corrections.md, "P-55"; from: explorations/coordinator/positions-audit/trial/evidence/judgement.md, "P-55"; by: coordinator, from the correction trial's proposal (its Opus writer), at his word of 2026-10-21T23:35:44Z ("Apply the corrections"), after his page comments of 20:06 to 23:33 UTC (positions-audit/evidence/curator-comments-2026-10-21.md); note: his answer "Item 15: 15a with (b) yes." at 2026-10-09T13:51:11.783Z, to item 1 of the coordinator's 11:56:38.064Z list of open decisions, whose full form is its 07:59:01.341Z text (ways 15a to 15d, and part (b)); each sentence checked against those two texts, which are what he saw, and kept; "item 15" relabelled array fork 1, since PLAN's item 15 now names another line. The last sentence leaves: forks 3 and 2 stand at defaults that climb batch 13 built while his answers are pending, review-queue items 30 and 31. Behind their building is his question of 2026-10-09T15:10:56.122Z, answering the coordinator's 14:56:40.919Z text, whether forks 2 and 3 can go into the next batch; the coordinator's 15:13:34.608Z reply put them into batch 13 at the judgements' defaults. Its "PLAN item 52" is now in PLAN-history.

- P-66 **The integer principle: Java's and the JVM's defaults stay where they make sense, and are corrected where they fail high performance or mathematical precision.** The team did so with rounding. A hardware behaviour that bytecode can express gets its own explicit spelling, as the specification's wrapping and saturating operators do. Where the JVM prevents a correct implementation, the project gives way for now. The peers to compare are Julia, X10, Swift and Rust.
  from: explorations/coordinator/positions-audit/trial/evidence/corrections.md, "P-66"; from: explorations/coordinator/positions-audit/trial/evidence/judgement.md, "P-66"; by: coordinator, from the correction trial's proposal (its Opus writer), at his word of 2026-10-21T23:35:44Z ("Apply the corrections"), after his page comments of 20:06 to 23:33 UTC (positions-audit/evidence/curator-comments-2026-10-21.md); note: the example "the sign of `GCD`" dropped: it is the coordinator's, added in its reply of 2026-09-22T07:18:38.151Z, and no Java default was corrected there: Java has no `GCD` operator, the nonnegative result is the specification's own (`Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex:553-555`), and the -4 was the team's interpreter. The rest kept. Source: 2026-09-22T07:17:57.713Z, his way of weighing the shift operators, answering the coordinator's 07:06:49.525Z text on Swift's masking shift: keep Java's and the JVM's defaults where they make sense, correct them where they fail high performance or mathematical precision, as the team did with rounding; give a hardware behaviour that bytecode can express its own spelling; give way where the JVM prevents it, for now; compare Julia and X10 beside Swift and Rust; he did not yet feel informed enough to decide the shifts themselves. On the trial's page, 2026-10-21 20:18 UTC, he called the entry, read whole, a correct and precise statement of his position; the drop is put to him as a question.

- P-85 **The array design's three questions are open, with no default, and return after the switch-over with array decisions A, B and D (PLAN item 13).** The three: a store per element type chosen by the factory, the size also kept as a field of the store, and a factory for a size known only at run time that returns the unsized `Array`. A is how `double[]` goes under a float vector, B where unboxed arithmetic happens, D sizes in the model's own types (P-87). The whole preparation for the array work is done before the switch-over. Array fork 3, on the third question's ground, is built at a default that waits for his review (review-queue item 30).
